ZTEXT00100002170b5ea4a9c0000000
Ezero owned runtime memory

runtime-prefix.zero.part reserves one2MiB block from the existing Zero hosted
arena. The generated initialization and runtime execute as one Zero program.
The normal Zero compiler still provides its unchanged4MiB hosted arena;
there are exactly64 declared variables including the eight prefix names.
Ezero calls use the following memory frames, not native recursive Zero calls.
The implementation is entirely original Zero code.

All offsets below are relative to ez_mem; all endpoints are exclusive.
0..262144       generated package; actual validated size is at most70000
262144..266240  256 global value slots
266240..528384  64 call frames,256 local value slots each
528384..532480  64 call metadata records,64 bytes each
532480..565248  2048 evaluation value slots
589824..851968  first262144-byte heap semispace
851968..1114112 second262144-byte heap semispace
1114112..1114272 twenty u64 decimal powers,10^0..10^19
1114368..1310976 bounded iterative display stack,8192 records of24bytes
1310976..1311040 decimal formatting scratch; at most20bytes used
Host and text convenience scratch in the remaining reservation is listed below.

Value cells are16bytes: u64 kind followed by u64 payload.
0 is uninitialized;1 is unsigned u64 number;2 is text object address;
3 is list object address;4 is typed phrase object address.
Variables automatically retain their current tagged value. No source-level
pointer arithmetic or manual allocation is exposed to Ezero programs.
Reading a variable that has not executed an assignment raises an error.

Objects are immutable and16-byte aligned. The header stores kind and length.
Text length is a byte count and is followed by UTF-8 bytes plus alignment
padding. SIZE counts Unicode scalar starts, not bytes or grapheme clusters;
UTF-8 validity is checked by the source frontend. Valid concatenations
preserve UTF-8. List/phrase length is a cell count followed by16-byte values.
Lists render with brackets and ' | ' separators; phrases use spaces and no
brackets. MAKE_PHRASE preserves typed parts, including all-text parts; the
frontend already merges consecutive literal text words into one text segment.
Collection INDEX is one-based:1 is first;0 and values above length fail.
Text indexing is not implemented. Lists and phrases can be nested.

Copying collection
Allocation checks the exact required object size. If the active semispace has
insufficient room, collection copies reachable objects into the other space.
Roots are all declared globals, all256 initialized/zeroed slots in every
active call frame, and the live evaluation stack. An allocating instruction
keeps its operands on that stack until collection completes, then reloads
all object addresses. Dead frames and stale stack entries are not roots.
Old objects receive a forwarding header (kind255, new address). Aliases reuse
that address, preserving identity without duplicate copies. A Cheney scan
rewrites references inside every copied list or phrase; text has no child
references. After scanning finishes, spaces swap and allocation resumes.
A live set plus the new object larger than262144bytes produces a managed
memory capacity error. This is automatic bounded collection, not unlimited
RAM or an OS virtual-memory allocator. No objects are individually freed.

Function frames
Call metadata offsets0,8,16 contain return instruction, caller evaluation
prefix length, and callee local count. Arguments become the first local
slots in order. All256 local slots are cleared on each call, and depth is
checked before frame access. Return restores the caller's prefix and appends
one result; references in active caller locals and intermediate expressions
remain GC roots. At most64 calls may be active. Main globals are separate.
The evaluation stack cannot consume values below the active call's prefix.

Execution guards
The runtime validates package header/size, section sizes, function entries
and counts before executing. Every instruction checks opcode, required stack
values, destinations, indices and available stack/frame/object capacity.
Arithmetic is unsigned and checked: addition/multiplication overflow and
negative subtraction fail. Comparisons return0/1. Numeric and text equality
compare contents; mixed numeric/text values differ; list/phrase equality is
rejected. Display walks nested collections iteratively and checks its stack.
These checks protect Ezero values in this hosted runtime; they are not an OS
process-isolation or hostile-native-code security boundary.

Errors print an ASCII diagnostic with the Ezero source line and exit65.
Already printed output may precede the diagnostic. Output is on stdout under
the current underlying Zero host-I/O contract. The native compiler command
does not publish outputs when compilation fails.
Native output I/O failures retain Zero's exit74 behavior.

Hosted validation
Initial own-compiler fixtures exercised nested lists, mixed text/u64 output,
UTF-8 typed phrases, one-based indexing, Unicode SIZE, recursive factorial
with a live caller expression, repeated4096-byte allocation/collection,
number overflow, negative subtraction and index-zero rejection.
Thirty independently designed package fixtures also passed when executed by
the runtime author: recursive collection with active caller/local/evaluation
roots, nested aliases, live-heap and call/evaluation-stack exhaustion, exact
64call capacity,256variables,16parameters, content equality and bad opcodes.
Broader frontend-to-runtime acceptance has a separate report. Guest execution
inside Zero OS is recorded separately in verification/system-0.10.0; the
historical component review above did not itself execute a guest image.

Host extension scratch in0.10 (no new declarations;64 variables retained)
1311040..1311104 host result/count/path-length metadata, persistent temp counter
1311360..1315456 NUL-terminated destination/read path,4096bytes
1315456..1319552 NUL-terminated sibling temporary path,4096bytes
1323008..1388545 external read scratch,65537bytes (one-byte overflow probe)
1392640..1393144 63argument byte lengths,8bytes each
These areas are outside both heap semispaces and iterative display storage.
Read bytes and argument length metadata are staged before the normal GC check.
File path operands stay on the evaluation stack throughout collection. The
read result is allocated/copied afterward; arguments reserve the entire list
and all strings before construction, with no collection during construction.
Native argument pointers are process-lifetime strings and are copied into
immutable managed text objects. No pointer into external staging escapes as
an Ezero value. Temporary-file sequence metadata is initialized before use.

Text convenience scratch (no new runtime declarations)
1311120..1311128 saved lines result count
1311128..1311136 saved join result UTF-8 byte count
1393152..1425920 up to 2048 line boundary pairs: byte offset and byte length
Offsets refer to the text argument's payload, not to its current heap address.
LINES reserves its complete list plus all independently aligned text objects
before collection. JOIN validates every list item and reserves one complete
text object. Both keep all input operands on the evaluation stack during GC,
then reload the updated pointers afterward. Construction performs no further
collection. Scratch lives outside heaps, frames, display state and host staging.
Repeated splits/joins cannot leave a pointer into scratch as an Ezero value.

File listing extension (same 64 runtime declarations)
WATCH/SEARCH/DISKS reuse external staging at 1323008..1388545, the saved count
at 1311120 and the 2048 boundary pairs at 1393152..1425920. Each staged name
must be nonempty, NUL-terminated and valid UTF-8; the final terminator must fit
inside the returned byte count. The complete list header, value cells and
aligned text objects are counted before collection. Offsets refer to external
staging, never movable heap addresses. The path operand remains rooted until
construction finishes; DISKS has no input operand. Result text is copied into
managed objects and no staging address escapes. Oversized, malformed or failed
listings stop with a source-line error instead of silently truncating results.

Guest READ_FILE and WRITE_FILE use whole-file service primitives; native
READ_FILE and exclusive WRITE_FILE retain their original handle/staging paths.
SAVE validates all text before invoking explicit create/replace. Numeric file
results are staged outside the heap and do not introduce additional GC roots.
